A||N||4||beachbomb||Need help(tach and coolant fan)!!!||||5||
Z||000000||beachbomb||05-19-2000||08:58 PM||||HI, i have a 1984 Indy fiero(4 cyclinder of course) and i have two nagging probs, 1)the tach reads about 3,000 rpm's faster than what the engine is really doing....any suggestions. 2)my electric fan does not come on and off according to engine temp, but does come on when A/A is on, i have replaced the collant temp switch, collant temp sensor and the collant fan relay to no aval. please help, thanks alot..jim||||reg||5
Z||000001||Zman302||05-20-2000||01:03 AM||||there is three coolant sensors on that motor.  one is for the gauge, one is for the fan and the coolant temp sensor sensor for the computer.  i havent had to replace any on the fiero yet but on all the a body gm cars i have worked on the sensor is located in the block toward the back.  if you have replaced it you might check with your parts store to find out what the temp that sensor kicks in.  the one they call for kick on around 220 but there is another one in the books that i have found that kicks on around 200.  If your car is anything like my 84, it almost never comes close to 220 to kick on the fan.||||reg||1
Z||000002||beachbomb||05-20-2000||08:04 AM||||thanks, i'll look for the third sensor, the two i have already replaced where near the termostat neck. i'll let you know if i find another one, thaks alot||||reg||1
Z||000003||William Federle||05-20-2000||08:53 AM||||On my 1984 Fiero the fan switch is located on the left hand end of the head pointed toward the front of the car. It's slightly hidden under a heat shield but the heat shield bends out of the way far enough to get a 23mm socket on.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000004||beachbomb||05-20-2000||05:49 PM||||I'll try looking there too. thanks for the info. i'll let you know if its there..jim<BR>||||reg||3
